 ((41
  ("40" 0.0625 0.0798 0.0460 0.0584 0.0584 0.0124 nil nil 0.0173)
  ("Chapter 1" 0.2050 0.2824 0.0460 0.0584 0.0584 0.0124 0.0072 0.2214 0.0615)
  ("Introduction" 0.2996 0.3935 0.0460 0.0584 0.0584 0.0124 nil 0.2147 0.0939)
  ("decision by the British government to end support for AI research in all but two universities." 0.2050 0.9062 0.0883 0.1018 0.1018 0.0135 0.0049 0.3015 0.0631)
  ("(Oral tradition paints a somewhat different and more colorful picture, with political ambitions" 0.2050 0.9062 0.1071 0.1206 0.1206 0.0135 0.0040 0.3589 0.0400)
  ("and personal animosities whose description is beside the point.)" 0.2050 0.6874 0.1259 0.1395 0.1395 0.0135 0.0047 0.2934 0.0273)
  ("A third difficulty arose because of some fundamental limitations on the basic structures" 0.2362 0.9062 0.1447 0.1583 0.1583 0.0135 0.0055 0.2983 0.0137)
  ("being used to generate intelligent behavior. For example, Minsky and Papert’s book Percep-" 0.2050 0.9062 0.1636 0.1771 0.1768 0.0135 0.0051 0.2838 0.0421)
  ("trons (1969) proved that, although perceptrons (a simple form of neural network) could be" 0.2050 0.9062 0.1824 0.1959 0.1956 0.0135 0.0061 0.2590 0.0381)
  ("shown to learn anything they were capable of representing, they could represent very little." 0.2050 0.9062 0.2012 0.2147 0.2147 0.0135 0.0058 0.2487 0.0490)
  ("In particular, a two-input perceptron could not be trained to recognize when its two inputs" 0.2050 0.9062 0.2200 0.2336 0.2336 0.0135 0.0062 0.2612 0.0158)
  ("were different. Although their results did not apply to more complex, multilayer networks," 0.2050 0.9062 0.2388 0.2524 0.2524 0.0135 0.0058 0.3097 0.0368)
  ("research funding for neural-net research soon dwindled to almost nothing. Ironically, the new" 0.2050 0.9062 0.2576 0.2712 0.2712 0.0135 0.0042 0.2932 0.0631)
  ("back-propagation learning algorithms that were to cause an enormous resurgence in neural-" 0.2050 0.9062 0.2765 0.2900 0.2900 0.0135 0.0055 0.2597 0.1324)
  ("net research in the late 1980s and again in the 2010s had already been developed in other" 0.2050 0.9062 0.2953 0.3088 0.3088 0.0135 0.0065 0.1945 0.0231)
  ("contexts in the early 1960s (Kelley, 1960; Bryson, 1962)." 0.2050 0.6378 0.3141 0.3276 0.3276 0.0135 0.0047 0.2856 0.0628)
  ("1.3.4 Expert systems (1969–1986)" 0.2050 0.5354 0.3414 0.3561 0.3561 0.0147 0.0076 0.2171 0.0469)
  ("Weak method" 0.0625 0.1485 0.4086 0.4184 0.4184 0.0098 0.0049 0.3393 0.0347)
  ("The picture of problem solving that had arisen during the first decade of AI research was of" 0.2050 0.9062 0.3683 0.3818 0.3818 0.0135 0.0053 0.2776 0.0295)
  ("a general-purpose search mechanism trying to string together elementary reasoning steps to" 0.2050 0.9062 0.3871 0.4007 0.4007 0.0135 0.0055 0.2694 0.0084)
  ("find complete solutions. Such approaches have been called weak methods because, although" 0.2050 0.9063 0.4057 0.4195 0.4194 0.0135 0.0043 0.2950 0.0295)
  ("general, they do not scale up to large or difficult problem instances. The alternative to weak" 0.2050 0.9062 0.4247 0.4383 0.4383 0.0135 0.0052 0.2830 0.0605)
  ("methods is to use more powerful, domain-specific knowledge that allows larger reasoning" 0.2050 0.9062 0.4436 0.4571 0.4571 0.0135 0.0067 0.2835 0.0642)
  ("steps and can more easily handle typically occurring cases in narrow areas of expertise. One" 0.2050 0.9062 0.4624 0.4759 0.4759 0.0135 0.0049 0.2756 0.0379)
  ("might say that to solve a hard problem, you have to almost know the answer already." 0.2050 0.8446 0.4812 0.4947 0.4947 0.0135 0.0047 0.3259 0.0442)
  ("The D ENDRAL program (Buchanan et al., 1969) was an early example of this approach." 0.2362 0.9063 0.5000 0.5136 0.5129 0.0135 0.0050 0.3193 0.0295)
  ("It was developed at Stanford, where Ed Feigenbaum (a former student of Herbert Simon)," 0.2050 0.9062 0.5188 0.5324 0.5324 0.0135 0.0064 0.2820 0.0116)
  ("Bruce Buchanan (a philosopher turned computer scientist), and Joshua Lederberg (a Nobel" 0.2050 0.9062 0.5377 0.5512 0.5512 0.0135 0.0059 0.2461 0.0452)
  ("laureate geneticist) teamed up to solve the problem of inferring molecular structure from the" 0.2050 0.9062 0.5565 0.5700 0.5700 0.0135 0.0049 0.3115 0.0599)
  ("information provided by a mass spectrometer. The input to the program consists of the ele-" 0.2050 0.9062 0.5753 0.5888 0.5888 0.0135 0.0055 0.2953 0.0894)
  ("mentary formula of the molecule (e.g., C_{6} H_{13} NO_{2} ) and the mass spectrum giving the masses" 0.2050 0.9062 0.5941 0.6091 0.6077 0.0135 0.0046 0.4024 0.0621)
  ("of the various fragments of the molecule generated when it is bombarded by an electron beam." 0.2050 0.9062 0.6129 0.6265 0.6265 0.0135 0.0038 0.3485 0.0158)
  ("For example, the mass spectrum might contain a peak at m = 15, corresponding to the mass" 0.2050 0.9062 0.6315 0.6453 0.6449 0.0135 0.0052 0.3329 0.0260)
  ("of a methyl (CH_{3} ) fragment." 0.2050 0.4205 0.6506 0.6655 0.6641 0.0135 0.0047 0.4170 0.0158)
  ("The naive version of the program generated all possible structures consistent with the" 0.2362 0.9062 0.6694 0.6829 0.6829 0.0135 0.0067 0.2427 0.0295)
  ("formula, and then predicted what mass spectrum would be observed for each, comparing this" 0.2050 0.9062 0.6882 0.7017 0.7017 0.0135 0.0045 0.3256 0.0647)
  ("with the actual spectrum. As one might expect, this is intractable for even moderate-sized" 0.2050 0.9062 0.7070 0.7206 0.7206 0.0135 0.0062 0.2912 0.0337)
  ("molecules. The D ENDRAL researchers consulted analytical chemists and found that they" 0.2050 0.9062 0.7258 0.7394 0.7387 0.0135 0.0073 0.2781 0.0815)
  ("worked by looking for well-known patterns of peaks in the spectrum that suggested common" 0.2050 0.9062 0.7447 0.7582 0.7582 0.0135 0.0046 0.3180 0.0564)
  ("substructures in the molecule. For example, the following rule is used to recognize a ketone" 0.2050 0.9062 0.7635 0.7770 0.7770 0.0135 0.0052 0.2877 0.0999)
  ("(C=O) subgroup (which weighs 28):" 0.2050 0.4808 0.7823 0.7958 0.7958 0.0135 0.0047 0.3085 0.0496)
  ("if M is the mass of the whole molecule and there are two peaks at x_{1} and x_{2} such that" 0.2483 0.8354 0.8072 0.8210 0.8194 0.0124 0.0043 0.3300 0.0106)
  ("(a) x_{1} + x_{2} = M + 28; (b) x_{1} − 28 is a high peak; (c) x_{2} − 28 is a high peak; and" 0.2483 0.7929 0.8230 0.8376 0.8360 0.0124 0.0043 0.4131 0.0192)
  ("(d) At least one of x_{1} and x_{2} is high" 0.2483 0.4929 0.8406 0.8542 0.8526 0.0124 0.0043 0.2940 0.0202)
  ("then there is a ketone subgroup." 0.2483 0.4710 0.8570 0.8695 0.8694 0.0124 0.0043 0.2524 0.0327)
  ("Recognizing that the molecule contains a particular substructure reduces the number of pos-" 0.2050 0.9062 0.8826 0.8962 0.8962 0.0135 0.0051 0.2750 0.0957)
  ("sible candidates enormously. According to its authors, D ENDRAL was powerful because it" 0.2050 0.9062 0.9015 0.9150 0.9144 0.0135 0.0060 0.2964 0.0358)
  ("embodied the relevant knowledge of mass spectroscopy not in the form of first principles but" 0.2050 0.9062 0.9203 0.9338 0.9338 0.0135 0.0047 0.3092 0.0747))
  (42
  ("Section 1.3" 0.4299 0.5096 0.0460 0.0584 0.0584 0.0124 0.0043 0.2834 0.0538)
  ("The History of Artificial Intelligence" 0.5269 0.7950 0.0460 0.0584 0.0584 0.0124 0.0043 0.3368 0.0288)
  ("in efficient “cookbook recipes” (Feigenbaum et al., 1971). The significance of D ENDRAL" 0.0938 0.7945 0.0883 0.1018 0.1012 0.0135 0.0064 0.2805 0.0147)
  ("was that it was the first successful knowledge-intensive system: its expertise derived from" 0.0938 0.7950 0.1071 0.1206 0.1203 0.0135 0.0066 0.2601 0.0293)
  ("large numbers of special-purpose rules. In 1971, Feigenbaum and others at Stanford began" 0.0938 0.7950 0.1259 0.1395 0.1395 0.0135 0.0058 0.2470 0.0375)
  ("the Heuristic Programming Project (HPP) to investigate the extent to which the new method-" 0.0938 0.7950 0.1447 0.1583 0.1583 0.0135 0.0046 0.3438 0.0231)
  ("ology of expert systems could be applied to other areas." 0.0938 0.5219 0.1634 0.1771 0.1770 0.0136 0.0047 0.2810 0.0431)
  ("The next major effort was the M YCIN system for diagnosing blood infections. With about" 0.1249 0.7950 0.1824 0.1959 0.1953 0.0135 0.0039 0.3862 0.0295)
  ("450 rules, M YCIN was able to perform as well as some experts, and considerably better than" 0.0938 0.7950 0.2012 0.2147 0.2141 0.0135 0.0049 0.3424 0.0284)
  ("junior doctors. It also contained two major differences from D ENDRAL. First, unlike the" 0.0938 0.7950 0.2200 0.2336 0.2329 0.0135 0.0067 0.3113 0.0452)
  ("D ENDRAL rules, no general theoretical model existed from which the M YCIN rules could be" 0.0942 0.7950 0.2388 0.2524 0.2517 0.0135 0.0047 0.3670 0.0137)
  ("deduced. They had to be acquired from extensive interviewing of experts. Second, the rules" 0.0938 0.7950 0.2576 0.2712 0.2712 0.0135 0.0052 0.2661 0.0678)
  ("had to reflect the uncertainty associated with medical knowledge. M YCIN incorporated a" 0.0938 0.7950 0.2765 0.2900 0.2894 0.0135 0.0070 0.3021 0.0273)
  ("calculus of uncertainty called certainty factors (see Chapter 13), which seemed (at the time)" 0.0938 0.7950 0.2951 0.3088 0.3087 0.0136 0.0045 0.3049 0.0621)
  ("to fit well with how doctors assessed the impact of evidence on the diagnosis." 0.0938 0.6799 0.3141 0.3276 0.3276 0.0135 0.0047 0.3164 0.0147)
  ("The first successful commercial expert system, R1, began operation at the Digital Equip-" 0.1249 0.7950 0.3329 0.3465 0.3465 0.0135 0.0045 0.3364 0.0295)
  ("ment Corporation (McDermott, 1982). The program helped configure orders for new com-" 0.0938 0.7950 0.3517 0.3653 0.3653 0.0135 0.0059 0.3125 0.0379)
  ("puter systems; by 1986, it was saving the company an estimated $40 million a year. By 1988," 0.0938 0.7950 0.3706 0.3841 0.3841 0.0135 0.0044 0.3431 0.0389)
  ("DEC’s AI group had 40 expert systems deployed, with more on the way. DuPont had 100 in" 0.0938 0.7950 0.3894 0.4029 0.4029 0.0135 0.0050 0.3244 0.0505)
  ("use and 500 in development. Nearly every major U.S. corporation had its own AI group and" 0.0938 0.7950 0.4082 0.4217 0.4217 0.0135 0.0050 0.3125 0.0252)
  ("was either using or investigating expert systems." 0.0938 0.4598 0.4270 0.4406 0.4406 0.0135 0.0047 0.2977 0.0293)
  ("The importance of domain knowledge was also apparent in the area of natural language" 0.1249 0.7950 0.4458 0.4594 0.4594 0.0135 0.0053 0.2815 0.0295)
  ("understanding. Despite the success of Winograd’s S HRDLU system, its methods did not ex-" 0.0938 0.7950 0.4646 0.4782 0.4775 0.0135 0.0054 0.3196 0.1120)
  ("tend to more general tasks: for problems such as ambiguity resolution it used simple rules" 0.0938 0.7950 0.4835 0.4970 0.4970 0.0135 0.0061 0.2826 0.0326)
  ("that relied on the tiny scope of the blocks world." 0.0938 0.4594 0.5023 0.5158 0.5158 0.0135 0.0047 0.2914 0.0284)
  ("Several researchers, including Eugene Charniak at MIT and Roger Schank at Yale, sug-" 0.1249 0.7950 0.5211 0.5346 0.5346 0.0135 0.0053 0.2790 0.0560)
  ("gested that robust language understanding would require general knowledge about the world" 0.0938 0.7950 0.5399 0.5535 0.5535 0.0135 0.0048 0.2701 0.0484)
  ("and a general method for using that knowledge. (Schank went further, claiming, “There is" 0.0938 0.7950 0.5587 0.5723 0.5723 0.0135 0.0061 0.2775 0.0273)
  ("no such thing as syntax,” which upset a lot of linguists but did serve to start a useful dis-" 0.0938 0.7950 0.5776 0.5911 0.5911 0.0135 0.0066 0.2315 0.0189)
  ("cussion.) Schank and his students built a series of programs (Schank and Abelson, 1977;" 0.0938 0.7950 0.5964 0.6099 0.6099 0.0135 0.0067 0.2302 0.0679)
  ("Wilensky, 1978; Schank and Riesbeck, 1981) that all had the task of understanding natural" 0.0938 0.7950 0.6152 0.6287 0.6287 0.0135 0.0059 0.2583 0.0751)
  ("language. The emphasis, however, was less on language per se and more on the problems of" 0.0938 0.7950 0.6340 0.6476 0.6472 0.0135 0.0049 0.3001 0.0731)
  ("representing and reasoning with the knowledge required for language understanding." 0.0938 0.7360 0.6528 0.6664 0.6664 0.0135 0.0047 0.2588 0.0936)
  ("The widespread growth of applications to real-world problems led to the development of" 0.1249 0.7950 0.6716 0.6852 0.6852 0.0135 0.0047 0.3167 0.0295)
  ("a wide range of representation and reasoning tools. Some were based on logic—for example," 0.0938 0.7950 0.6905 0.7040 0.7040 0.0135 0.0044 0.3323 0.0084)
  ("the Prolog language became popular in Europe and Japan, and the P LANNER family in the" 0.0938 0.7950 0.7093 0.7228 0.7222 0.0135 0.0059 0.2792 0.0231)
  ("United States. Others, following Minsky’s idea of frames (1975), adopted a more structured" 0.0938 0.7950 0.7279 0.7416 0.7415 0.0135 0.0049 0.3370 0.0516)
  ("approach, assembling facts about particular object and event types and arranging the types" 0.0938 0.7950 0.7469 0.7605 0.7605 0.0135 0.0062 0.2275 0.0741)
  ("into a large taxonomic hierarchy analogous to a biological taxonomy." 0.0938 0.6181 0.7657 0.7793 0.7793 0.0135 0.0047 0.2922 0.0295)
  ("In 1981, the Japanese government announced the “Fifth Generation” project, a 10-year" 0.1249 0.7950 0.7846 0.7981 0.7981 0.0135 0.0058 0.2395 0.0158)
  ("plan to build massively parallel, intelligent computers running Prolog. The budget was to" 0.0938 0.7950 0.8034 0.8169 0.8169 0.0135 0.0066 0.2771 0.0326)
  ("exceed a $1.3 billion in today’s money. In response, the United States formed the Micro-" 0.0938 0.7950 0.8222 0.8357 0.8357 0.0135 0.0065 0.2860 0.0523)
  ("electronics and Computer Technology Corporation (MCC), a consortium designed to assure" 0.0938 0.7950 0.8410 0.8546 0.8546 0.0135 0.0053 0.3010 0.0820)
  ("national competitiveness. In both cases, AI was part of a broad effort, including chip design" 0.0938 0.7950 0.8598 0.8734 0.8734 0.0135 0.0052 0.2858 0.0610)
  ("and human-interface research. In Britain, the Alvey report reinstated the funding removed by" 0.0938 0.7950 0.8787 0.8922 0.8922 0.0135 0.0044 0.3040 0.0273)
  ("the Lighthill report. However, none of these projects ever met its ambitious goals in terms of" 0.0938 0.7950 0.8975 0.9110 0.9110 0.0135 0.0048 0.3330 0.0231)
  ("new AI capabilities or economic impact." 0.0938 0.4005 0.9163 0.9298 0.9298 0.0135 0.0047 0.3495 0.0311)
  ("41" 0.9202 0.9375 0.0460 0.0584 0.0584 0.0124 nil nil 0.0173)
  ("Expert systems" 0.8090 0.9016 0.1651 0.1750 0.1750 0.0098 0.0049 0.2725 0.0404)
  ("Certainty factor" 0.8090 0.9054 0.2969 0.3067 0.3067 0.0098 0.0049 0.2362 0.0561)
  ("Frames" 0.8090 0.8534 0.7308 0.7406 0.7406 0.0098 nil 0.2903 0.0443))
  (43
  ("42" 0.0625 0.0798 0.0460 0.0584 0.0584 0.0124 nil nil 0.0173)
  ("Chapter 1" 0.2050 0.2824 0.0460 0.0584 0.0584 0.0124 0.0072 0.2214 0.0615)
  ("Introduction" 0.2996 0.3935 0.0460 0.0584 0.0584 0.0124 nil 0.2147 0.0939)
  ("Overall, the AI industry boomed from a few million dollars in 1980 to billions of dollars" 0.2362 0.9062 0.0883 0.1018 0.1018 0.0135 0.0048 0.3489 0.0613)
  ("in 1988, including hundreds of companies building expert systems, vision systems, robots," 0.2050 0.9062 0.1071 0.1206 0.1206 0.0135 0.0061 0.2681 0.0147)
  ("and software and hardware specialized for these purposes." 0.2050 0.6454 0.1259 0.1395 0.1395 0.0135 0.0047 0.2585 0.0273)
  ("Soon after that came a period called the “AI winter,” in which many companies fell by the" 0.2362 0.9062 0.1447 0.1583 0.1583 0.0135 0.0041 0.3660 0.0389)
  ("wayside as they failed to deliver on extravagant promises. It turned out to be difficult to build" 0.2050 0.9062 0.1636 0.1771 0.1771 0.0135 0.0045 0.3063 0.0619)
  ("and maintain expert systems for complex domains, in part because the reasoning methods" 0.2050 0.9062 0.1824 0.1959 0.1959 0.0135 0.0066 0.2734 0.0273)
  ("used by the systems broke down in the face of uncertainty and in part because the systems" 0.2050 0.9062 0.2012 0.2147 0.2147 0.0135 0.0059 0.2527 0.0347)
  ("could not learn from experience." 0.2050 0.4514 0.2200 0.2336 0.2336 0.0135 0.0047 0.2830 0.0421)
  ("1.3.5 The return of neural networks (1986–present)" 0.2050 0.6995 0.2517 0.2665 0.2665 0.0147 0.0076 0.2147 0.0469)
  ("Connectionist" 0.0625 0.1457 0.3770 0.3868 0.3868 0.0098 nil 0.2572 0.0832)
  ("In the mid-1980s at least four different groups reinvented the back-propagation learning" 0.2050 0.9062 0.2801 0.2938 0.2937 0.0136 0.0070 0.2268 0.0158)
  ("algorithm first developed in the early 1960s. The algorithm was applied to many learning" 0.2050 0.9062 0.2991 0.3126 0.3126 0.0135 0.0065 0.2817 0.0737)
  ("problems in computer science and psychology, and the widespread dissemination of the re-" 0.2050 0.9062 0.3179 0.3314 0.3314 0.0135 0.0057 0.2722 0.0705)
  ("sults in the collection Parallel Distributed Processing (Rumelhart and McClelland, 1986)" 0.2050 0.9062 0.3367 0.3503 0.3499 0.0135 0.0072 0.2859 0.0347)
  ("caused great excitement." 0.2050 0.3914 0.3555 0.3691 0.3691 0.0135 0.0047 0.2791 0.0515)
  ("These so-called connectionist models were seen by some as direct competitors both to" 0.2362 0.9062 0.3742 0.3879 0.3878 0.0135 0.0058 0.2797 0.0452)
  ("the symbolic models promoted by Newell and Simon and to the logicist approach of Mc-" 0.2050 0.9062 0.3932 0.4067 0.4067 0.0135 0.0067 0.2984 0.0231)
  ("Carthy and others. It might seem obvious that at some level humans manipulate symbols—in" 0.2050 0.9062 0.4120 0.4255 0.4255 0.0135 0.0044 0.3734 0.0515)
  ("fact, the anthropologist Terrence Deacon’s book The Symbolic Species (1997) suggests that" 0.2050 0.9063 0.4308 0.4443 0.4440 0.0135 0.0058 0.2394 0.0329)
  ("this is the defining characteristic of humans. Against this, Geoff Hinton, a leading figure" 0.2050 0.9062 0.4496 0.4632 0.4628 0.0135 0.0067 0.2854 0.0274)
  ("in the resurgence of neural networks in the 1980s and 2010s, has described symbols as the" 0.2050 0.9062 0.4684 0.4820 0.4820 0.0135 0.0059 0.2375 0.0147)
  ("“luminiferous aether of AI”—a reference to the non-existent medium through which many" 0.2050 0.9062 0.4873 0.5008 0.5008 0.0135 0.0061 0.3169 0.1052)
  ("19th-century physicists believed that electromagnetic waves propagated. Certainly, many" 0.2050 0.9062 0.5061 0.5196 0.5196 0.0135 0.0074 0.2662 0.0968)
  ("concepts that we name in language fail, on closer inspection, to have the kind of logically" 0.2050 0.9062 0.5249 0.5384 0.5384 0.0135 0.0063 0.2520 0.0663)
  ("defined necessary and sufficient conditions that early AI researchers hoped to capture in ax-" 0.2050 0.9062 0.5437 0.5573 0.5573 0.0135 0.0053 0.2431 0.0558)
  ("iomatic form. It may be that connectionist models form internal concepts in a more fluid" 0.2050 0.9062 0.5625 0.5761 0.5761 0.0135 0.0067 0.3105 0.0568)
  ("and imprecise way that is better suited to the messiness of the real world. They also have" 0.2050 0.9062 0.5813 0.5949 0.5949 0.0135 0.0063 0.2729 0.0273)
  ("the capability to learn from examples—they can compare their predicted output value to the" 0.2050 0.9062 0.6002 0.6137 0.6137 0.0135 0.0052 0.3228 0.0231)
  ("true value on a problem and modify their parameters to decrease the difference, making them" 0.2050 0.9062 0.6190 0.6325 0.6325 0.0135 0.0044 0.3191 0.0295)
  ("more likely to perform well on future examples." 0.2050 0.5685 0.6378 0.6514 0.6514 0.0135 0.0047 0.3512 0.0389)
  ("1.3.6 Probabilistic reasoning and machine learning (1987–present)" 0.2050 0.8372 0.6695 0.6843 0.6843 0.0147 0.0076 0.2587 0.0469)
  ("The brittleness of expert systems led to a new, more scientific approach incorporating proba-" 0.2050 0.9062 0.6981 0.7116 0.7116 0.0135 0.0048 0.2937 0.0295)
  ("bility rather than Boolean logic, machine learning rather than hand-coding, and experimental" 0.2050 0.9062 0.7169 0.7304 0.7304 0.0135 0.0046 0.2961 0.0400)
  ("results rather than philosophical claims.^{14} It became more common to build on existing theo-" 0.2050 0.9062 0.7330 0.7492 0.7492 0.0135 0.0044 0.3345 0.0495)
  ("ries than to propose brand-new ones, to base claims on rigorous theorems or solid experimen-" 0.2050 0.9062 0.7545 0.7681 0.7681 0.0135 0.0042 0.3208 0.0273)
  ("tal methodology (Cohen, 1995) rather than on intuition, and to show relevance to real-world" 0.2050 0.9062 0.7733 0.7869 0.7869 0.0135 0.0051 0.2949 0.0189)
  ("applications rather than toy examples." 0.2050 0.4923 0.7921 0.8057 0.8057 0.0135 0.0047 0.2841 0.0915)
  ("Shared benchmark problem sets became the norm for demonstrating progress, including" 0.2362 0.9062 0.8110 0.8245 0.8245 0.0135 0.0050 0.3028 0.0526)
  ("the UC Irvine repository for machine learning data sets, the International Planning Compe-" 0.2050 0.9062 0.8298 0.8433 0.8433 0.0135 0.0057 0.2868 0.0231)
  ("^{14} Some have characterized this change as a victory of the neats—those who think that AI theories should be" 0.2050 0.9062 0.8600 0.8724 0.8723 0.0111 0.0050 0.2738 0.0121)
  ("grounded in mathematical rigor—over the scruffies—those who would rather try out lots of ideas, write some" 0.2050 0.9062 0.8763 0.8876 0.8875 0.0111 0.0050 0.3377 0.0588)
  ("programs, and then assess what seems to be working. Both approaches are important. A shift toward neatness" 0.2050 0.9062 0.8917 0.9028 0.9028 0.0111 0.0048 0.2868 0.0627)
  ("implies that the field has reached a level of stability and maturity. The present emphasis on deep learning may" 0.2050 0.9062 0.9069 0.9180 0.9180 0.0111 0.0048 0.2847 0.0458)
  ("represent a resurgence of the scruffies." 0.2050 0.4438 0.9221 0.9332 0.9332 0.0111 0.0039 0.2287 0.0571))
  (44
  ("Section 1.3" 0.4299 0.5096 0.0460 0.0584 0.0584 0.0124 0.0043 0.2834 0.0538)
  ("The History of Artificial Intelligence" 0.5269 0.7950 0.0460 0.0584 0.0584 0.0124 0.0043 0.3368 0.0288)
  ("43" 0.9202 0.9375 0.0460 0.0584 0.0584 0.0124 nil nil 0.0173)
  ("tition for planning algorithms, the LibriSpeech corpus for speech recognition, the MNIST" 0.0938 0.7950 0.0883 0.1018 0.1018 0.0135 0.0067 0.2794 0.0400)
  ("data set for handwritten digit recognition, ImageNet and COCO for image object recogni-" 0.0938 0.7950 0.1071 0.1206 0.1206 0.0135 0.0062 0.3089 0.0316)
  ("tion, SQ UAD for natural language question answering, the WMT competition for machine" 0.0938 0.7950 0.1259 0.1395 0.1388 0.0135 0.0058 0.3582 0.0342)
  ("translation, and the International SAT Competitions for Boolean satisfiability solvers." 0.0938 0.7400 0.1447 0.1583 0.1583 0.0135 0.0047 0.3040 0.0847)
  ("AI was founded in part as a rebellion against the limitations of existing fields like control" 0.1249 0.7950 0.1636 0.1771 0.1771 0.0135 0.0045 0.3178 0.0200)
  ("theory and statistics, but in this period it embraced the positive results of those fields. As" 0.0938 0.7950 0.1824 0.1959 0.1959 0.0135 0.0065 0.2620 0.0484)
  ("David McAllester (1998) put it:" 0.0938 0.3343 0.2012 0.2147 0.2147 0.0135 0.0047 0.3779 0.0459)
  ("In the early period of AI it seemed plausible that new forms of symbolic computation," 0.1370 0.7517 0.2295 0.2419 0.2419 0.0124 0.0058 0.2988 0.0144)
  ("e.g., frames and semantic networks, made much of classical theory obsolete. This led to" 0.1370 0.7517 0.2461 0.2585 0.2585 0.0124 0.0048 0.3169 0.0293)
  ("a form of isolationism in which AI became largely separated from the rest of computer" 0.1370 0.7517 0.2627 0.2751 0.2751 0.0124 0.0054 0.3154 0.0077)
  ("science. This isolationism is currently being abandoned. There is a recognition that" 0.1370 0.7517 0.2794 0.2917 0.2917 0.0124 0.0069 0.2595 0.0552)
  ("machine learning should not be isolated from information theory, that uncertain reasoning" 0.1370 0.7517 0.2960 0.3083 0.3083 0.0124 0.0037 0.3201 0.0586)
  ("should not be isolated from stochastic modeling, that search should not be isolated from" 0.1370 0.7517 0.3126 0.3249 0.3249 0.0124 0.0048 0.2790 0.0461)
  ("classical optimization and control, and that automated reasoning should not be isolated" 0.1370 0.7517 0.3292 0.3415 0.3415 0.0124 0.0056 0.2616 0.0586)
  ("from formal methods and static analysis." 0.1370 0.4185 0.3458 0.3581 0.3581 0.0124 0.0043 0.3403 0.0336)
  ("The field of speech recognition illustrates the pattern. In the 1970s, a wide variety of different" 0.0938 0.7950 0.3746 0.3881 0.3881 0.0135 0.0041 0.2991 0.0295)
  ("architectures and approaches were tried. Many of these were rather ad hoc and fragile, and" 0.0938 0.7950 0.3934 0.4069 0.4069 0.0135 0.0056 0.2660 0.0968)
  ("worked on only a few carefully selected examples. In the 1980s, approaches using hidden" 0.0938 0.7950 0.4120 0.4257 0.4256 0.0135 0.0059 0.2499 0.0564)
  ("Markov models (HMMs) came to dominate the area. Two aspects of HMMs are relevant." 0.0938 0.7950 0.4308 0.4446 0.4444 0.0135 0.0060 0.3640 0.0648)
  ("First, they are based on a rigorous mathematical theory. This allowed speech researchers to" 0.0938 0.7950 0.4498 0.4634 0.4634 0.0135 0.0055 0.2728 0.0395)
  ("build on several decades of mathematical results developed in other fields. Second, they are" 0.0938 0.7950 0.4686 0.4822 0.4822 0.0135 0.0053 0.2695 0.0386)
  ("generated by a process of training on a large corpus of real speech data. This ensures that the" 0.0938 0.7950 0.4875 0.5010 0.5010 0.0135 0.0046 0.2611 0.0736)
  ("performance is robust, and in rigorous blind tests HMMs improved their scores steadily. As" 0.0938 0.7950 0.5063 0.5198 0.5198 0.0135 0.0053 0.3330 0.0957)
  ("a result, speech technology and the related field of handwritten character recognition made" 0.0938 0.7950 0.5251 0.5386 0.5386 0.0135 0.0059 0.2515 0.0084)
  ("the transition to widespread industrial and consumer applications. Note that there was no" 0.0938 0.7950 0.5439 0.5575 0.5575 0.0135 0.0067 0.2696 0.0231)
  ("scientific claim that humans use HMMs to recognize speech; rather, HMMs provided a math-" 0.0938 0.7950 0.5627 0.5763 0.5763 0.0135 0.0043 0.3969 0.0684)
  ("ematical framework for understanding and solving the problem. We will see in Section 1.3.8," 0.0938 0.7950 0.5816 0.5951 0.5951 0.0135 0.0044 0.3482 0.0642)
  ("however, that deep learning has rather upset this comfortable narrative." 0.0938 0.6301 0.6004 0.6139 0.6139 0.0135 0.0047 0.2864 0.0680)
  ("1988 was an important year for the connection between AI and other fields, including" 0.1249 0.7950 0.6192 0.6327 0.6327 0.0135 0.0064 0.2637 0.0379)
  ("statistics, operations research, decision theory, and control theory. Judea Pearl’s (1988) Prob-" 0.0938 0.7950 0.6380 0.6516 0.6512 0.0135 0.0042 0.2677 0.0700)
  ("abilistic Reasoning in Intelligent Systems led to a new acceptance of probability and decision" 0.0938 0.7950 0.6568 0.6704 0.6700 0.0135 0.0045 0.2945 0.0610)
  ("theory in AI. Pearl’s development of Bayesian networks yielded a rigorous and efficient" 0.0938 0.7950 0.6755 0.6892 0.6891 0.0136 0.0072 0.2493 0.0484)
  ("formalism for representing uncertain knowledge as well as practical algorithms for proba-" 0.0938 0.7950 0.6945 0.7080 0.7080 0.0135 0.0066 0.2763 0.0779)
  ("bilistic reasoning. Chapters 12, 13, 14, 15, and 18 cover this area, in addition to more recent" 0.0938 0.7950 0.7133 0.7268 0.7268 0.0135 0.0050 0.2878 0.0516)
  ("developments that have greatly increased the expressive power of probabilistic formalisms;" 0.0938 0.7950 0.7321 0.7456 0.7456 0.0135 0.0059 0.2737 0.1045)
  ("Chapter 21 describes methods for learning Bayesian networks and related models from data." 0.0938 0.7937 0.7509 0.7645 0.7645 0.0135 0.0047 0.3029 0.0600)
  ("A second major contribution in 1988 was Rich Sutton’s work connecting reinforcement" 0.1249 0.7950 0.7697 0.7833 0.7833 0.0135 0.0054 0.2930 0.0137)
  ("learning—which had been used in Arthur Samuel’s checker-playing program in the 1950s—" 0.0938 0.7950 0.7886 0.8021 0.8021 0.0135 0.0049 0.3174 0.1273)
  ("to the theory of Markov decision processes (MDPs) developed in the field of operations re-" 0.0938 0.7950 0.8074 0.8209 0.8209 0.0135 0.0056 0.2896 0.0147)
  ("search. A flood of work followed connecting AI planning research to MDPs, and the field of" 0.0938 0.7950 0.8262 0.8397 0.8397 0.0135 0.0048 0.3278 0.0531)
  ("reinforcement learning found applications in robotics and process control as well as acquiring" 0.0938 0.7950 0.8450 0.8586 0.8586 0.0135 0.0040 0.2989 0.1062)
  ("deep theoretical foundations." 0.0938 0.3131 0.8638 0.8774 0.8774 0.0135 0.0047 0.2364 0.0358)
  ("One consequence of AI’s newfound appreciation for data, statistical modeling, optimiza-" 0.1249 0.7950 0.8826 0.8962 0.8962 0.0135 0.0046 0.3164 0.0316)
  ("tion, and machine learning was the gradual reunification of subfields such as computer vision," 0.0938 0.7950 0.9015 0.9150 0.9150 0.0135 0.0040 0.3187 0.0342)
  ("robotics, speech recognition, multiagent systems, and natural language processing that had" 0.0938 0.7950 0.9203 0.9338 0.9338 0.0135 0.0061 0.2544 0.0658)
  ("Hidden Markov" 0.8090 0.9025 0.4288 0.4386 0.4386 0.0098 0.0049 0.3266 0.0432)
  ("models" 0.8090 0.8517 0.4385 0.4483 0.4483 0.0098 nil 0.3535 0.0427)
  ("Bayesian network" 0.8090 0.9149 0.6772 0.6870 0.6870 0.0098 0.0049 0.2498 0.0532))
  (45
  ("44" 0.0625 0.0798 0.0460 0.0584 0.0584 0.0124 nil nil 0.0173)
  ("Chapter 1" 0.2050 0.2824 0.0460 0.0584 0.0584 0.0124 0.0072 0.2214 0.0615)
  ("Introduction" 0.2996 0.3935 0.0460 0.0584 0.0584 0.0124 nil 0.2147 0.0939)
  ("become somewhat separate from core AI. The process of reintegration has yielded signifi-" 0.2050 0.9062 0.0883 0.1018 0.1018 0.0135 0.0064 0.2756 0.0589)
  ("cant benefits both in terms of applications—for example, the deployment of practical robots" 0.2050 0.9062 0.1071 0.1206 0.1206 0.0135 0.0052 0.3275 0.0316)
  ("expanded greatly during this period—and in a better theoretical understanding of the core" 0.2050 0.9062 0.1259 0.1395 0.1395 0.0135 0.0066 0.2545 0.0723)
  ("problems of AI." 0.2050 0.3255 0.1447 0.1583 0.1583 0.0135 0.0047 0.3588 0.0705)
  ("1.3.7 Big data (2001–present)" 0.2050 0.4959 0.1752 0.1899 0.1899 0.0147 0.0076 0.2134 0.0469)
  ("Big data" 0.0625 0.1151 0.2236 0.2334 0.2334 0.0098 0.0049 0.2968 0.0207)
  ("Remarkable advances in computing power and the creation of the World Wide Web have" 0.2050 0.9062 0.2032 0.2167 0.2167 0.0135 0.0070 0.2907 0.0915)
  ("facilitated the creation of very large data sets—a phenomenon sometimes known as big data." 0.2050 0.9062 0.2218 0.2356 0.2354 0.0135 0.0043 0.3464 0.0756)
  ("These data sets include trillions of words of text, billions of images, and billions of hours of" 0.2050 0.9062 0.2408 0.2544 0.2544 0.0135 0.0051 0.2910 0.0452)
  ("speech and video, as well as vast amounts of genomic data, vehicle tracking data, clickstream" 0.2050 0.9062 0.2596 0.2732 0.2732 0.0135 0.0043 0.3119 0.0515)
  ("data, social network data, and so on." 0.2050 0.4799 0.2785 0.2920 0.2920 0.0135 0.0047 0.2918 0.0363)
  ("This has led to the development of learning algorithms specially designed to take advan-" 0.2362 0.9062 0.2973 0.3108 0.3108 0.0135 0.0048 0.2891 0.0337)
  ("tage of very large data sets. Often, the vast majority of examples in such data sets are un-" 0.2050 0.9062 0.3161 0.3296 0.3293 0.0135 0.0061 0.2624 0.0316)
  ("labeled; for example, in Yarowsky’s (1995) influential work on word-sense disambiguation," 0.2050 0.9062 0.3349 0.3485 0.3481 0.0135 0.0054 0.2958 0.0610)
  ("occurrences of a word such as “plant” are not labeled in the data set to indicate whether they" 0.2050 0.9062 0.3537 0.3673 0.3673 0.0135 0.0048 0.2747 0.0904)
  ("refer to flora or factory. With large enough data sets, however, suitable learning algorithms" 0.2050 0.9062 0.3726 0.3861 0.3861 0.0135 0.0057 0.2929 0.0357)
  ("can achieve an accuracy of over 96% on the task of identifying which sense was intended in a" 0.2050 0.9062 0.3914 0.4049 0.4049 0.0135 0.0043 0.3094 0.0263)
  ("sentence. Moreover, Banko and Brill (2001) argued that the improvement in performance ob-" 0.2050 0.9062 0.4102 0.4237 0.4237 0.0135 0.0042 0.3327 0.0699)
  ("tained from increasing the size of the data set by two or three orders of magnitude outweighs" 0.2050 0.9062 0.4290 0.4426 0.4426 0.0135 0.0047 0.3127 0.0463)
  ("any improvement that can be obtained from tweaking the algorithm." 0.2050 0.7207 0.4478 0.4614 0.4614 0.0135 0.0047 0.3344 0.0271)
  ("A similar phenomenon seems to occur in computer vision tasks such as filling in holes in" 0.2362 0.9062 0.4666 0.4802 0.4802 0.0135 0.0045 0.3402 0.0137)
  ("photographs—holes caused either by damage or by the removal of ex-friends. Hays and Efros" 0.2050 0.9062 0.4855 0.4990 0.4990 0.0135 0.0039 0.3362 0.1526)
  ("(2007) developed a clever method for doing this by blending in pixels from similar images;" 0.2050 0.9062 0.5043 0.5178 0.5178 0.0135 0.0055 0.2936 0.0505)
  ("they found that the technique worked poorly with a database of only thousands of images but" 0.2050 0.9062 0.5231 0.5366 0.5366 0.0135 0.0045 0.2961 0.0323)
  ("crossed a threshold of quality with millions of images. Soon after, the availability of tens of" 0.2050 0.9062 0.5419 0.5555 0.5555 0.0135 0.0052 0.3006 0.0568)
  ("millions of images in the ImageNet database (Deng et al., 2009) sparked a revolution in the" 0.2050 0.9063 0.5607 0.5743 0.5739 0.0135 0.0054 0.3101 0.0621)
  ("field of computer vision." 0.2050 0.3912 0.5796 0.5931 0.5931 0.0135 0.0047 0.3211 0.0337)
  ("The availability of big data and the shift towards machine learning helped AI recover" 0.2362 0.9062 0.5984 0.6119 0.6119 0.0135 0.0068 0.2497 0.0295)
  ("commercial attractiveness (Havenstein, 2005; Halevy et al., 2009). Big data was a crucial fac-" 0.2050 0.9063 0.6172 0.6307 0.6304 0.0135 0.0038 0.3451 0.0894)
  ("tor in the 2011 victory of IBM’s Watson system over human champions in the Jeopardy! quiz" 0.2050 0.9062 0.6360 0.6496 0.6496 0.0135 0.0044 0.3530 0.0210)
  ("game, an event that had a major impact on the public’s perception of AI." 0.2050 0.7528 0.6548 0.6684 0.6684 0.0135 0.0047 0.3310 0.0457)
  ("1.3.8 Deep learning (2011–present)" 0.2050 0.5454 0.6853 0.7000 0.7000 0.0147 0.0076 0.2284 0.0469)
  ("Deep learning" 0.0625 0.1464 0.7149 0.7247 0.7247 0.0098 0.0049 0.2968 0.0313)
  ("The term deep learning refers to machine learning using multiple layers of simple, adjustable" 0.2050 0.9062 0.7131 0.7268 0.7267 0.0135 0.0039 0.3477 0.0295)
  ("computing elements. Experiments were carried out with such networks as far back as the" 0.2050 0.9062 0.7321 0.7456 0.7456 0.0135 0.0066 0.2821 0.0810)
  ("1970s, and in the form of convolutional neural networks they found some success in hand-" 0.2050 0.9063 0.7507 0.7645 0.7644 0.0136 0.0048 0.2889 0.0500)
  ("written digit recognition in the 1990s (LeCun et al., 1995). It was not until 2011, however," 0.2050 0.9062 0.7697 0.7833 0.7829 0.0135 0.0057 0.2936 0.0537)
  ("that deep learning methods really took off. This occurred first in speech recognition and then" 0.2050 0.9062 0.7886 0.8021 0.8021 0.0135 0.0046 0.2832 0.0284)
  ("in visual object recognition." 0.2050 0.4165 0.8074 0.8209 0.8209 0.0135 0.0047 0.2646 0.0147)
  ("In the 2012 ImageNet competition, which required classifying images into one of a thou-" 0.2362 0.9062 0.8262 0.8397 0.8397 0.0135 0.0044 0.3323 0.0158)
  ("sand categories (armadillo, bookshelf, corkscrew, etc.), a deep learning system created in" 0.2050 0.9062 0.8450 0.8586 0.8586 0.0135 0.0071 0.2478 0.0347)
  ("Geoffrey Hinton’s group at the University of Toronto (Krizhevsky et al., 2013) demonstrated" 0.2050 0.9063 0.8638 0.8774 0.8770 0.0135 0.0047 0.3163 0.0676)
  ("a dramatic improvement over previous systems, which were based largely on handcrafted" 0.2050 0.9062 0.8826 0.8962 0.8962 0.0135 0.0069 0.2697 0.0084)
  ("features. Since then, deep learning systems have exceeded human performance on some vi-" 0.2050 0.9062 0.9015 0.9150 0.9150 0.0135 0.0054 0.2713 0.0647)
  ("sion tasks (and lag behind in some other tasks). Similar gains have been reported in speech" 0.2050 0.9062 0.9203 0.9338 0.9338 0.0135 0.0055 0.2612 0.0316))
  (46
  ("Section 1.4" 0.5510 0.6307 0.0460 0.0584 0.0584 0.0124 0.0043 0.2834 0.0538)
  ("The State of the Art" 0.6480 0.7950 0.0460 0.0584 0.0584 0.0124 0.0043 0.3234 0.0288)
  ("45" 0.9202 0.9375 0.0460 0.0584 0.0584 0.0124 nil nil 0.0173)
  ("recognition, machine translation, medical diagnosis, and game playing. The use of a deep" 0.0938 0.7950 0.0883 0.1018 0.1018 0.0135 0.0063 0.2700 0.0910)
  ("network to represent the evaluation function contributed to A LPHAG O’s victories over the" 0.0938 0.7950 0.1071 0.1206 0.1200 0.0135 0.0066 0.2935 0.0619)
  ("leading human Go players (Silver et al., 2016, 2017, 2018)." 0.0938 0.5448 0.1259 0.1395 0.1391 0.0135 0.0047 0.3107 0.0558)
  ("These remarkable successes have led to a resurgence of interest in AI among students," 0.1249 0.7950 0.1447 0.1583 0.1583 0.0135 0.0061 0.2464 0.0452)
  ("companies, investors, governments, the media, and the general public. It seems that every" 0.0938 0.7950 0.1636 0.1771 0.1771 0.0135 0.0063 0.2809 0.0857)
  ("week there is news of a new AI application approaching or exceeding human performance," 0.0938 0.7950 0.1824 0.1959 0.1959 0.0135 0.0058 0.2725 0.0400)
  ("often accompanied by speculation of either accelerated success or a new AI winter." 0.0938 0.7239 0.2012 0.2147 0.2147 0.0135 0.0047 0.2922 0.0389)
  ("Deep learning relies heavily on powerful hardware. Whereas a standard computer CPU" 0.1249 0.7950 0.2200 0.2336 0.2336 0.0135 0.0055 0.3008 0.0400)
  ("can do 10^{9} or 10^{10} operations per second. a deep learning algorithm running on specialized" 0.0938 0.7950 0.2361 0.2524 0.2524 0.0135 0.0054 0.2402 0.0263)
  ("hardware (e.g., GPU, TPU, or FPGA) might consume between 10^{14} and 10^{17} operations per" 0.0938 0.7950 0.2549 0.2712 0.2712 0.0135 0.0051 0.3253 0.0703)
  ("second, mostly in the form of highly parallelized matrix and vector operations. Of course," 0.0938 0.7950 0.2765 0.2900 0.2900 0.0135 0.0061 0.2811 0.0573)
  ("deep learning also depends on the availability of large amounts of training data, and on a few" 0.0938 0.7950 0.2953 0.3088 0.3088 0.0135 0.0046 0.2857 0.0358)
  ("algorithmic tricks (see Chapter 22)." 0.0938 0.3625 0.3141 0.3276 0.3276 0.0135 0.0047 0.3073 0.0873)
  ("1.4 The State of the Art" 0.0938 0.3816 0.3569 0.3745 0.3745 0.0177 0.0091 0.2410 0.0350)
  ("Stanford University’s One Hundred Year Study on AI (also known as AI100) convenes panels" 0.0938 0.7950 0.3940 0.4076 0.4076 0.0135 0.0040 0.3238 0.0652)
  ("of experts to provide reports on the state of the art in AI. Their 2016 report (Stone et al.," 0.0938 0.7950 0.4128 0.4264 0.4260 0.0135 0.0066 0.2362 0.0158)
  ("2016; Grosz and Stone, 2018) concludes that “Substantial increases in the future uses of AI" 0.0938 0.7950 0.4317 0.4452 0.4452 0.0135 0.0054 0.2572 0.0431)
  ("applications, including more self-driving cars, healthcare diagnostics and targeted treatment," 0.0938 0.7950 0.4505 0.4640 0.4640 0.0135 0.0049 0.2807 0.0963)
  ("and physical assistance for elder care can be expected” and that “Society is now at a crucial" 0.0938 0.7950 0.4693 0.4828 0.4828 0.0135 0.0053 0.2391 0.0273)
  ("juncture in determining how to deploy AI-based technologies in ways that promote rather than" 0.0938 0.7950 0.4881 0.5017 0.5017 0.0135 0.0037 0.3457 0.0621)
  ("hinder democratic values such as freedom, equality, and transparency.” AI100 also produces" 0.0938 0.7950 0.5069 0.5205 0.5205 0.0135 0.0050 0.2741 0.0484)
  ("an AI Index at aiindex.org to help track progress. Some highlights from the 2018 and" 0.0938 0.7950 0.5256 0.5394 0.5392 0.0135 0.0064 0.2493 0.0179)
  ("2019 reports (comparing to a year 2000 baseline unless otherwise stated):" 0.0938 0.6513 0.5446 0.5581 0.5581 0.0135 0.0047 0.2772 0.0379)
  ("• Publications: AI papers increased 20-fold between 2010 and 2019 to about 20,000 a" 0.1217 0.7950 0.5731 0.5867 0.5867 0.0135 0.0063 0.2270 0.0066)
  ("year. The most popular category was machine learning. (Machine learning papers" 0.1370 0.7950 0.5920 0.6055 0.6055 0.0135 0.0075 0.2826 0.0363)
  ("in arXiv.org doubled every year from 2009 to 2017.) Computer vision and natural" 0.1370 0.7950 0.6108 0.6243 0.6243 0.0135 0.0075 0.2547 0.0147)
  ("language processing were the next most popular." 0.1370 0.5054 0.6296 0.6432 0.6432 0.0135 0.0047 0.2868 0.0684)
  ("• Sentiment: About 70% of news articles on AI are neutral, but articles with positive tone" 0.1217 0.7950 0.6530 0.6665 0.6665 0.0135 0.0043 0.3561 0.0066)
  ("increased from 12% in 2016 to 30% in 2018. The most common issues are ethical: data" 0.1370 0.7950 0.6718 0.6853 0.6853 0.0135 0.0043 0.3603 0.0715)
  ("privacy and algorithm bias." 0.1370 0.3430 0.6906 0.7041 0.7041 0.0135 0.0047 0.3007 0.0556)
  ("• Students: Course enrollment increased 5-fold in the U.S. and 16-fold internationally" 0.1217 0.7950 0.7140 0.7275 0.7275 0.0135 0.0066 0.2636 0.0066)
  ("from a 2010 baseline. AI is the most popular specialization in Computer Science." 0.1370 0.7536 0.7328 0.7463 0.7463 0.0135 0.0047 0.3223 0.0368)
  ("• Diversity: AI Professors worldwide are about 80% male, 20% female. Similar numbers" 0.1217 0.7950 0.7561 0.7697 0.7697 0.0135 0.0043 0.3776 0.0066)
  ("hold for Ph.D. students and industry hires." 0.1370 0.4574 0.7749 0.7885 0.7885 0.0135 0.0047 0.2939 0.0337)
  ("• Conferences: Attendance at NeurIPS increased 800% since 2012 to 13,500 attendees." 0.1217 0.7950 0.7983 0.8118 0.8118 0.0135 0.0056 0.2574 0.0066)
  ("Other conferences are seeing annual growth of about 30%." 0.1370 0.5809 0.8171 0.8307 0.8307 0.0135 0.0047 0.2951 0.0431)
  ("• Industry: AI startups in the U.S. increased 20-fold to over 800." 0.1217 0.6125 0.8405 0.8540 0.8540 0.0135 0.0047 0.2930 0.0066)
  ("• Internationalization: China publishes more papers per year than the U.S. and about" 0.1217 0.7950 0.8638 0.8774 0.8774 0.0135 0.0073 0.2422 0.0066)
  ("as many as all of Europe. However, in citation-weighted impact, U.S. authors are 50%" 0.1370 0.7950 0.8826 0.8962 0.8962 0.0135 0.0051 0.3315 0.0158)
  ("ahead of Chinese authors. Singapore, Brazil, Australia, Canada, and India are the fastest" 0.1370 0.7950 0.9015 0.9150 0.9150 0.0135 0.0038 0.3144 0.0442)
  ("growing countries in terms of the number of AI hires." 0.1370 0.5426 0.9203 0.9338 0.9338 0.0135 0.0047 0.3391 0.0627)
  ("AI Index" 0.8090 0.8604 0.5284 0.5382 0.5382 0.0098 0.0049 0.3107 0.0139))
  (47
  ("46" 0.0625 0.0798 0.0460 0.0584 0.0584 0.0124 nil nil 0.0173)
  ("Chapter 1" 0.2050 0.2824 0.0460 0.0584 0.0584 0.0124 0.0072 0.2214 0.0615)
  ("Introduction" 0.2996 0.3935 0.0460 0.0584 0.0584 0.0124 nil 0.2147 0.0939)
  ("• Vision: Error rates for object detection (as achieved in LSVRC, the Large-Scale Visual" 0.2330 0.9063 0.0883 0.1018 0.1018 0.0135 0.0048 0.3003 0.0066)
  ("Recognition Challenge) improved from 28% in 2010 to 2% in 2017, exceeding human" 0.2483 0.9063 0.1071 0.1206 0.1206 0.0135 0.0051 0.3235 0.0926)
  ("performance. Accuracy on open-ended visual question answering (VQA) improved" 0.2483 0.9063 0.1259 0.1395 0.1395 0.0135 0.0073 0.2648 0.1004)
  ("from 55% to 68% since 2015, but lags behind human performance at 83%." 0.2483 0.8139 0.1447 0.1583 0.1583 0.0135 0.0047 0.3506 0.0368)
  ("• Speed: Training time for the image recognition task dropped by a factor of 100 in just" 0.2330 0.9063 0.1680 0.1815 0.1815 0.0135 0.0052 0.2788 0.0066)
  ("the past two years. The amount of computing power used in top AI applications is" 0.2483 0.9063 0.1868 0.2003 0.2003 0.0135 0.0070 0.2746 0.0231)
  ("doubling every 3.4 months." 0.2483 0.4553 0.2056 0.2192 0.2192 0.0135 0.0047 0.2935 0.0673)
  ("• Language: Accuracy on question answering, as measured by F1 score on the Stanford" 0.2330 0.9063 0.2289 0.2424 0.2424 0.0135 0.0053 0.2555 0.0066)
  ("Question Answering Dataset (SQ UAD), increased from 60 to 95 from 2015 to 2019; on" 0.2483 0.9062 0.2477 0.2612 0.2606 0.0135 0.0041 0.3827 0.0684)
  ("the SQ UAD 2 variant, progress was faster, going from 62 to 90 in just one year. Both" 0.2483 0.9062 0.2665 0.2800 0.2794 0.0135 0.0053 0.3359 0.0231)
  ("scores exceed human-level performance." 0.2483 0.5564 0.2853 0.2989 0.2989 0.0135 0.0047 0.2644 0.0473)
  ("• Human benchmarks: By 2019, AI systems had reportedly met or exceeded human-" 0.2330 0.9063 0.3086 0.3221 0.3221 0.0135 0.0072 0.2625 0.0066)
  ("level performance in chess, Go, poker, Pac-Man, Jeopardy!, ImageNet object detection," 0.2483 0.9063 0.3274 0.3409 0.3409 0.0135 0.0044 0.3327 0.0361)
  ("speech recognition in a limited domain, Chinese-to-English translation in a restricted" 0.2483 0.9063 0.3462 0.3597 0.3597 0.0135 0.0062 0.2782 0.0515)
  ("domain, Quake III, Dota 2, StarCraft II, various Atari games, skin cancer detection," 0.2483 0.9063 0.3650 0.3786 0.3786 0.0135 0.0068 0.2904 0.0616)
  ("prostate cancer detection, protein folding, and diabetic retinopathy diagnosis." 0.2483 0.8326 0.3838 0.3974 0.3974 0.0135 0.0047 0.2562 0.0600)
  ("When (if ever) will AI systems achieve human-level performance across a broad variety" 0.2362 0.9062 0.4122 0.4257 0.4257 0.0135 0.0052 0.3236 0.0452)
  ("of tasks? Ford (2018) interviews AI experts and finds a wide range of target years, from 2029" 0.2050 0.9062 0.4310 0.4446 0.4446 0.0135 0.0043 0.3149 0.0158)
  ("to 2200, with a mean of 2099. In a similar survey (Grace et al., 2017) 50% of respondents" 0.2050 0.9062 0.4498 0.4634 0.4630 0.0135 0.0057 0.3011 0.0147)
  ("thought this could happen by 2066, although 10% thought it could happen as early as 2025," 0.2050 0.9062 0.4686 0.4822 0.4822 0.0135 0.0053 0.2613 0.0579)
  ("and a few said “never.” The experts were also split on whether we need fundamental new" 0.2050 0.9062 0.4875 0.5010 0.5010 0.0135 0.0063 0.2655 0.0273)
  ("breakthroughs or just refinements on current approaches. But don’t take their predictions" 0.2050 0.9062 0.5063 0.5198 0.5198 0.0135 0.0069 0.2404 0.1084)
  ("too seriously; as Philip Tetlock (2017) demonstrates in the area of predicting world events," 0.2050 0.9062 0.5251 0.5386 0.5386 0.0135 0.0059 0.2570 0.0242)
  ("experts are no better than amateurs." 0.2050 0.4740 0.5439 0.5575 0.5575 0.0135 0.0047 0.2832 0.0544)
  ("How will future AI systems operate? We can’t yet say. As detailed in this section, the field" 0.2362 0.9062 0.5627 0.5763 0.5763 0.0135 0.0037 0.3887 0.0363)
  ("has adopted several stories about itself—first the bold idea that intelligence by a machine was" 0.2050 0.9062 0.5816 0.5951 0.5951 0.0135 0.0042 0.3387 0.0252)
  ("even possible, then that it could be achieved by encoding expert knowledge into logic, then" 0.2050 0.9062 0.6004 0.6139 0.6139 0.0135 0.0056 0.2486 0.0350)
  ("that probabilistic models of the world would be the main tool, and most recently that machine" 0.2050 0.9062 0.6192 0.6327 0.6327 0.0135 0.0042 0.3619 0.0284)
  ("learning would induce models that might not be based on any well-understood theory at all." 0.2050 0.9062 0.6380 0.6516 0.6516 0.0135 0.0053 0.2916 0.0621)
  ("The future will reveal what model comes next." 0.2050 0.5574 0.6568 0.6704 0.6704 0.0135 0.0047 0.3482 0.0295)
  ("What can AI do today? Perhaps not as much as some of the more optimistic media" 0.2362 0.9062 0.6756 0.6892 0.6892 0.0135 0.0073 0.2988 0.0410)
  ("articles might lead one to believe, but still a great deal. Here are some examples:" 0.2050 0.8149 0.6945 0.7080 0.7080 0.0135 0.0047 0.3263 0.0547)
  ("Robotic vehicles: The history of robotic vehicles stretches back to radio-controlled cars" 0.2362 0.9062 0.7131 0.7268 0.7267 0.0136 0.0050 0.2604 0.0631)
  ("of the 1920s, but the first demonstrations of autonomous road driving without special guides" 0.2050 0.9062 0.7321 0.7456 0.7456 0.0135 0.0049 0.2997 0.0158)
  ("occurred in the 1980s (Kanade et al., 1986; Dickmanns and Zapp, 1987). After success-" 0.2050 0.9062 0.7509 0.7645 0.7641 0.0135 0.0069 0.2490 0.0662)
  ("ful demonstrations of driving on dirt roads in the 132-mile DARPA Grand Challenge in" 0.2050 0.9062 0.7697 0.7833 0.7833 0.0135 0.0078 0.2773 0.0210)
  ("2005 (Thrun, 2006) and on streets with traffic in the 2007 Urban Challenge, the race to de-" 0.2050 0.9062 0.7886 0.8021 0.8021 0.0135 0.0056 0.2699 0.0379)
  ("velop self-driving cars began in earnest. In 2018, Waymo test vehicles passed the landmark" 0.2050 0.9062 0.8074 0.8209 0.8209 0.0135 0.0054 0.2827 0.0418)
  ("of 10 million miles driven on public roads without a serious accident, with the human driver" 0.2050 0.9062 0.8262 0.8397 0.8397 0.0135 0.0050 0.3249 0.0158)
